3. docs/research/lingq/known_lexicons/<lang>.json for de es fr it pt — the usable known-vocabulary lexicon: terms at the status tier you determined means 'known/learned', lowercased, deduplicated, single words and multi-word separately, with per-tier counts in a header object. These will constrain sentence generation ('sentences built only from words the owner knows').
4. docs/research/lingq/dormant_expressions/<lang>.json for de es fr it pt — multi-word terms (contains a space; for zh skip) at high status WITH their original fragment and hints — activation candidates for expression drills. Sort by status desc then term.
5. docs/research/lingq/REPORT.md — findings: real status semantics in this account, fragment quality (are the sentences complete/usable?), per-language dormant-value sizing, data quality issues (encoding, duplicates, truncated fragments), the 5 dormant-language (sv nl da no + zh) picture, and a short 'what is feasible with this data' section. Numbers everywhere.

